# TMS9995 Instruction Set Manual

> Reference: [TMS9900 CPU (Nouspikel)](https://www.unige.ch/medecine/nouspikel/ti99/tms9900.htm), [Ninerpedia Instruction Set comparison](https://ninerpedia.org/wiki/Instruction_Set_of_all_TMS99xxx_CPUs)

## Overview

The **TMS9995** is a 16-bit microprocessor in the TMS9900 family. It is **opcode-compatible with the TMS9900** and adds a small set of new instructions. The TMS9995 was intended for the TI-99/2, TI-99/8, Tomy Tutor, and Geneve 9640.

| Item | TMS9900 | TMS9995 |
|------|---------|---------|
| Data bus (external) | 16-bit | **8-bit** |
| Internal ALU / registers | 16-bit | 16-bit |
| On-chip RAM | none | **256 bytes** (workspace can map here) |
| Instruction prefetch | no | **yes** (~3× faster than TMS9900) |
| Base instruction set | 69 instructions | TMS9900 + **MPYS, DIVS, LST, LWP** |

### Architectural notes

- **Memory-to-memory architecture**: operands are usually in RAM, not inside the CPU.
- **Workspace registers R0–R15** live in external (or on-chip) RAM; the **Workspace Pointer (WP)** selects the base address.
- **Three internal registers**: PC (Program Counter), ST (Status), WP (Workspace Pointer).
- **Bit numbering**: MSB is **bit 0**, LSB is **bit 15** (TI convention). Hex notation uses `>xxxx`.
- **No hardware stack pointer**; subroutines use BL/BLWP and workspace registers R11–R15.
- **Word-aligned** instructions; length is 1, 2, or 3 words.

---

## Workspace Layout

When WP points to address `WP`, workspace register `Rn` is at address `WP + 2*n`.

| Register | Typical role (convention) |
|----------|---------------------------|
| R0 | General / indexed addressing sentinel |
| R1–R10 | General purpose |
| R11 | Subroutine return address (BL) / XOP source EA |
| R12 | **CRU base address** (Communications Register Unit I/O) |
| R13 | Saved WP (after BLWP / interrupt) |
| R14 | Saved PC (after BLWP / interrupt) |
| R15 | Saved ST (after BLWP / interrupt) |

After **BLWP** or an **interrupt**, the new workspace receives:

- R13 ← old WP
- R14 ← old PC
- R15 ← old ST

**RTWP** restores ST, PC, WP from R15, R14, R13.

---

## Status Register (ST)

| Bit | Name | Meaning |
|-----|------|---------|
| 0 | L> | Logical greater than |
| 1 | A> | Arithmetic greater than |
| 2 | EQ | Equal (zero result for logical ops) |
| 3 | C | Carry |
| 4 | OV | Overflow |
| 5 | OP | Odd parity |
| 6 | X | Set during XOP execution |
| 7–11 | — | Reserved (0) |
| 12–15 | IM | **Interrupt mask** (LIMI sets this) |

Conditional jumps test these flags. **LIMI** loads bits 12–15 only.

